      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Luc.T",
      "description": "Vertical configured pylon on the 380 kV power line between Mercator substation (Kruibeke) and Horta substation (Zomergem) in Sint-Niklaas (Belgium).\nThis type of pylon is the most common in Belgium. The small traverse on top carries two thin cables: ground wires bolted uninsulated directly onto the pylon. The conductors are hanging on the traverses by means of insulator chains made of separate glass dishes. Their length depends on the voltage. For 380 kV more than 20 dishes are being used. The conductors consist of two separate wires (bundle conductors). Because the power plants generate 3-phase alternating current, the number of conductors on a high-voltage pylon is always 3 or a multiple of 3. The 380 kV powerline Mercator–Horta is an overhead connection from 1974. The line spans 49 km in the Oost-Vlaanderen province from substation Mercator in Kruibeke to high-voltage substation Horta in Zomergem.\nNotice the balls on the right ground wire. These serve as a beacon and radar reflector for aviation and shipping. The assertion that these spheres are intended to warn birds is a fable, but on some bird migration routes special \"bird spirals\" are attached to the ground wires to increase visibility.",
